course: myths
lesson: 27
title: Everyday sayings and grown-ups' rules
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: "Turn it off and on again!" says Nan, and the telly works. "Batteries in the fridge!" says Granddad. "You can't get burnt when it's cloudy," says your big sister, and comes home pink.
explain: Grown-ups' rules are passed on like recipes: some were true once, some are half true, and some were never true but sounded sensible.
explain: Restarting really does fix many glitches, because it clears out a computer's muddle; but it can't fix a cracked screen or a broken part.
explain: The cloudy-day rule is the dangerous one: most of the sun's burning rays pass straight through clouds, so you need sun cream anyway.
explain: When you think a grown-up's rule is wrong, ask kindly "how does that work?" and look it up together; some of them, like money being grubby, turn out to be truer than you think.
fact: Turning a device off and on again often fixes software glitches (small faults in how it runs) because it clears its working memory, but it cannot fix broken parts.
fact: Switching a light on uses only a tiny extra burst of power, far less than leaving it on, so turning lights off when you leave a room saves electricity.
fact: Swallowed chewing gum does not stick your intestines (your guts) together; it passes through in a day or two.
fact: Modern batteries do not need to be kept in the fridge; a cool, dry place at room temperature is best, and the fridge can make them damp.
fact: Investigations have found no clear evidence that phones secretly record conversations to choose adverts; adverts mostly come from searches, location and other data, but people still argue about it.
fact: You do not have to wait 24 hours to report a missing person; tell the police straight away.
fact: The "sea" you hear in a seashell is the sound around you echoing inside the shell; a cup held to your ear does the same.
fact: Slouching does not give children a hunchback (a back bent into a hump); moving about and strong muscles matter more, though sitting badly for hours can cause aches.
fact: Studies have found that banknotes and coins can carry more germs than a toilet seat, which is cleaned often, so washing hands after handling money is sensible.
fact: Hot plastic bottles can let tiny amounts of chemicals into water, but studies found amounts far below safety limits; a refilled warm bottle can grow germs, so wash it.
fact: You can get sunburnt on a cloudy day, because most of the sun's burning ultraviolet light passes through clouds.
fact: Some grown-ups' rules are true, some half true and some false, so each one is worth checking on its own.
exercise: Why does turning a device off and on often fix it? | It clears out its working memory | It shakes the dust out of the wires | It fixes cracked screens | It recharges the battery fully
exercise: Does leaving the lights on save electricity compared with switching them off and on? | No, switching off saves more | Yes, switching on uses a huge burst | Yes, but only with old bulbs | It makes no difference either way
exercise: Can swallowed gum stick your intestines together? | No, it passes through in a day or two | Yes, it glues them for seven years | Yes, if you swallow more than one piece | Only if it is bubble gum
exercise: Where should you keep batteries? | In a cool, dry place at room temperature | In the fridge, so that they last for years | In the freezer, for the best power and life | In a warm sunny window to keep them charged
exercise: Do investigations show phones secretly listen to choose adverts? | No clear evidence; adverts come mostly from searches and other data | Yes, every phone records you all day | Yes, but only when the phone is switched off | No, phones have no microphones at all
exercise: When should you report a missing person? | Straight away | After 24 hours | After a week | Only if they have been gone overnight
exercise: What do you really hear in a seashell? | Sounds around you echoing in the shell | The sea, trapped in the shell for ever | Your heart beating very loudly | The animal that once lived in it
exercise: Does slouching give children a hunchback? | No, but sitting badly for hours can cause aches | Yes, after just one week | Yes, if you slouch while reading | No, and sitting badly never causes any problems at all
exercise: Which can carry more germs, money or a toilet seat? | Money can carry more, as studies have found | The toilet seat, every single time it's tested | They always have exactly the same | Neither has any germs at all
exercise: What do studies say about water in a hot plastic bottle? | Tiny amounts of chemicals, far below safety limits | It becomes deadly poison within an hour | It turns into plastic itself | It cannot change in any way at all
exercise: Can you get sunburnt on a cloudy day? | Yes, most burning rays pass through clouds | No, clouds block all of the sun's burning rays | Only if it is raining at the very same time | Only in the middle of winter, when the sun is low
exercise: Why is a refilled warm water bottle a problem? | It can grow germs, so it should be washed | The plastic slowly melts and you swallow it | The water inside slowly turns into vinegar | It can explode when you twist the lid open
exercise: Which grown-up's rule here turns out to be true? | Wash your hands after handling money | Wait 24 hours to report someone missing | Keep batteries in the fridge | Clouds stop you getting sunburnt
exercise: What is a kind way to question a grown-up's rule? | "How does that work? Shall we look it up?" | "That's stupid, everyone knows it's wrong" | "I'm not doing it until you prove it" | Say nothing and ignore the rule forever
test: "turn it off n on again nan, that's all u ever say!!" When does her trick NOT work? | When a part is broken, like a cracked screen | When the device is new | When it's switched on at night | It always works, on everything
test: Dad leaves the hall light on all evening "to save electricity". What would actually save more? | Switching it off when no one is there | Leaving it on all night as well | Switching it on and off every single minute | Using two lights instead of one
test: "OMG i swallowed my gum, my guts will stick!!" What do you tell your mate? | It'll pass through in a day or two | Drink some cooking oil to unstick them | Go to hospital right now, without waiting | It'll stay stuck in there for seven years
test: Granddad puts the new batteries in the fridge. What could happen? | They could get damp, and it doesn't help modern batteries | They will last a hundred years | They will freeze and explode | They will charge themselves overnight
test: "my phone heard me say trainers and now it's all trainer ads, it's listening!!" What's a fairer explanation? | The ads more likely came from searches and other data; people still argue | It definitely recorded you and sent it to a shop | Phones read your mind through the screen | Trainers ads appear for everyone always
test: Your friend's brother hasn't come home and an adult says to wait 24 hours. What's right? | Tell the police straight away | Wait the full 24 hours first | Wait until next week | Only tell the school on Monday
test: You hold a cup to your ear and hear the "sea". What does this show? | The sound is the room echoing, not the sea | Cups also hold sea water | The sea is very close to your house, so you hear it | Your ears are making the sea noise
test: Mum says "sit up straight or you'll grow a hunchback". What's the honest part? | Sitting badly for hours can cause aches | You'll grow a hump in a week | Your spine will freeze bent | There's nothing honest in that saying at all
test: "money is dirtier than a toilet seat, lol grandpa" Who's right? | Grandpa: studies found money can carry more germs | You: toilet seats are always dirtier | Neither: money has no germs | Nobody knows, because it has never ever been tested
test: "it's cloudy, don't need suncream" says your big sister at the beach. What will probably happen? | She can still get sunburnt | She will stay perfectly safe | She'll get burnt only if it rains | She'll get a tan but never a burn
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning exposure to ultraviolet radiation under an overcast sky. | The greater part of burning ultraviolet radiation penetrates the cloud | Cloud cover wholly prevents sunburn | Sunburn occurs under cloud solely during rainfall | Ultraviolet radiation is intensified a hundredfold by cloud
exam: It is given that a person has failed to return home. Determine the appropriate interval before a report is made to the police. | No interval; the report should be made immediately | An interval of twenty-four hours | An interval of one week | An interval of forty-eight hours
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the restarting of an electronic device frequently remedies a malfunction. | It clears the device's working memory | It removes dust from the internal wiring | It repairs damaged physical components | It restores the battery to full charge
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sound perceived when a seashell is held to the ear? | Ambient sound resonating within the shell | The sound of the sea retained within the shell | The sound of the listener's blood alone | The sound of the shell's former occupant
